## Refactored (safe) code:
```cpp
#include <memory>
#include <iostream>

class Resource {
public:
    Resource() { std::cout << "Resource acquired\n"; }
    ~Resource() { std::cout << "Resource released\n"; }
    void doWork() { std::cout << "Working...\n"; }
};

class Container {
    std::unique_ptr<Resource> res1;
    std::unique_ptr<Resource> res2;
public:
    Container() 
        : res1(std::make_unique<Resource>()), 
          res2(std::make_unique<Resource>()) {}

    // No need for destructor - smart pointers handle cleanup

    void process() {
        res1->doWork();
        res2->doWork();
    }
};

void function() {
    auto c = std::make_unique<Container>();
    c->process();
    // No delete needed - automatically cleaned up
}
```

## Key improvements:

1. **`std::unique_ptr`** replaces raw owning pointers
2. **`std::make_unique`** creates objects safely (exception-safe)
3. **No manual `delete`** - automatic cleanup when pointers go out of scope
4. **No explicit destructor** needed in Container class
5. **Clear ownership semantics** - unique_ptr clearly shows exclusive ownership

