The issue is that you're using `await` on each API call individually, which forces them to execute sequentially. To run them in parallel, you need to start all the promises first, then await them all together using `Promise.all()`.

## The Buggy Code (Sequential):
```javascript
async function fetchData() {
  const data1 = await fetch('https://api1.com/data');
  const data2 = await fetch('https://api2.com/data');
  const data3 = await fetch('https://api3.com/data');

  return { data1: await data1.json(), data2: await data2.json(), data3: await data3.json() };
}
```

## The Corrected Code (Parallel):
```javascript
async function fetchData() {
  // Start all requests in parallel (no await here)
  const promise1 = fetch('https://api1.com/data');
  const promise2 = fetch('https://api2.com/data');
  const promise3 = fetch('https://api3.com/data');

  // Wait for all promises to resolve
  const [response1, response2, response3] = await Promise.all([promise1, promise2, promise3]);

  // Parse JSON responses (these can also run in parallel if desired)
  const [data1, data2, data3] = await Promise.all([
    response1.json(),
    response2.json(),
    response3.json()
  ]);

  return { data1, data2, data3 };
}
```

## Alternative (More Concise):
```javascript
async function fetchData() {
  const [data1, data2, data3] = await Promise.all([
    fetch('https://api1.com/data').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('https://api2.com/data').then(r => r.json()),
    fetch('https://api3.com/data').then(r => r.json())
  ]);

  return { data1, data2, data3 };
}
```

## Key Differences:

**❌ Sequential (Buggy):**
- Each `await` pauses execution until that specific promise resolves
- API 2 doesn't start until API 1 finishes
- Total time = sum of all API response times

**✅ Parallel (Fixed):**
- All fetch calls are initiated immediately without `await`
- `Promise.all()` waits for ALL to complete
- Total time = slowest single API response time

